<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13078"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, April 21, 2005 - 5:39 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Gold Star was what children were taught to work toward during his growing up. A gold star on a paper, a finished piece of music for a lesson, behavioral charts, etc.  <BR> <BR>I still believe that Rader has some minimal brain injury as evidenced by his sentence construction. That is where his defense team should go to present a defense. If I were on the defense team, I would show that despite his cleverness, his brain cannot make the connections properly, his brain injury is a permanent condition, it was given no remediation while in public school, and therefore, he is handicapped from a neurological standpoint. It would be interesting to see what his performance would be on an intelligence test. The Wechsler Adult Intelligence Test is a good test of personality and the Rorschach is an excellent intelligence test as well. He is an overachiever, from a school work standpoint.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
