<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13093"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 22, 2005 - 6:12 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Itty: I guess that it could go either way. His problem may be an &#39;expressive&#39; one. Visual and/or auditory sequencing/memory. I know someone who worked on the moon missions with NASA. A real spelling problem, despite his profound technical writing abilities. He spelled &#39;play&#39; as &#39;paly&#39;. All the visual elements and letter respresentations for sounds were there but in the wrong sequence. As far as I could tell, he did not have a problem with reading or reading comprehension. That is why a battery of testing on Rader as well as other people like him could be enlightening. Neuropsychologists often administer tests for surgeons to pinpoint a tumor prior to surgery or locate that part of the brain that is affected. Of course, modern imaging is probably making some of their skills obsolete. <BR> <BR>I am glad to see that others noticed a part missing from the note. I thought that I was having tumor issues myself. Has anyone else written Rader? I am considering giving it a shot and asking to write his story. Nothing ventured, nothing gained. He makes so few public appearances that we will have to watch closely to see any changes in physical appearance, like weight loss. Should he really have colon cancer, it should eventually begin to show on someone of his size.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
