<!--Post: 13165--><!--Time: 1114446096-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13165"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, April 25, 2005 - 9:21 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->His comments about how someone knows something about him hints that he is not as lucid at times as we would think. If questioned about their client, his lawyers might indicate that Rader does not at times appear fully cognizant of what is going on with him, i.e., the charges, mounting a defense. His &#39;world&#39; may be much smaller than we thought. He may also be developing &#39;intensive care-itis&#39;, where one has been so confined and without normal stimulation that they drift in and out of reality. <BR> <BR>I still maintain that he would be in special education if in the school system today. What must his term papers have read? Perhaps he had someone helping with his work, doing rewrites, checking spelling, etc. His thoughts are disjointed. He substitutes one similar word for another. His handwriting itself is atrocious. I could not have gotten out of first grade with that kind of work. I think that he schmoozed his way through school, substituting his affability and good boy approach to compensate for his difficulty with school work. This facade was essentially a wall that repelled feelings of inadequacy and he has maintained it to this day. His work required concrete things to do which provided him with the intensive structure that he required to function. A head injury in later life might account for a change in writing/expression. <BR> <BR>All that OC behavior and rigidity in his code enforcer work and ADT work could result in constipation, on a lighter note.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
