<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13225"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, April 26, 2005 - 9:08 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I would think that he would object to an insanity defense, as he would have to admit that he was not in his right mind for the last 31 years. He would have to admit that all his work and accomplishments were a sham by a crazy person. He is too vain for that. <BR> <BR>The defense is going to have to show some overriding, beyond his control, physical anomaly which prevented him from discerning right from wrong during his &#39;killer&#39; times while allowing him to be a good worker, church member, etc. He will be pissing on a hot wire. An insanity defense will not fly. Just because his acts were horrendous to the rest of the world does not mean that they were insane to him. Quite the contrary, he tooted his own horn numerous times. He would hate being in a facility for the criminally insane. He will meet much worse individuals than himself and soon assume the fetal position. <BR> <BR>The only thing that I can think of would be to find that he has Jacksonian epilepsy and committed these acts while in such a state. However, the time he spent at the scene, the prep, the pics, all scream of a person fully cognizant and in control of his faculties. He is a dead man. Even if released to freedom, he won&#39;t stay alive for long. <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
